  <h2>Three roles, one namespace</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>HotLoop part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Edge node</td><td><a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a></td><td>Polls the machines and publishes them as Sparkplug B, with a disk queue for outages.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hub</td><td>The embedded broker, or your own</td><td>Carries the namespace. The embedded one is on by default and fits a single plant.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Primary host</td><td><a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a></td><td>Tracks every node's births and deaths, keeps the history, raises the alarms, and writes readings out to your database.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>States, not guesses</h2>
  <p>As a primary host the Gateway publishes a retained STATE birth with a matching will, so edge nodes can see the host drop and not infer it from a timeout. It tracks each node's sequence number and asks for a rebirth when one goes missing. That is the part of Sparkplug most integrations skip, and it is why a value in the namespace means something.</p>

  <h2>Start with one machine</h2>
  <p>Put one Edge Relay on one machine, point it at the broker, and watch the node come alive in the Gateway. Add the next machine the same way. A pushed device file applies live, so adding a device is not a visit to the box.</p>
